Some thoughts -

Change the castle wand of wishing into an artifact or a one-of-a-kind
item similar to the Bell of Opening or the Book of the Dead - thus
disallowing random generation of WoWs.

A #rubbed magic lamp could always leave behind a hostile djinni (with
drastically pumped up stats / abilities, esp. E-word ignoring)
regardless of whether or not a wish is granted.

All artifacts in bones files should revert to their base item class,
similar to how the game currently handles same-class quest artifacts
in bones files.

All items in a bones file should be cursed with 100% probability.

Scale maximum randomly generated armor enchantment to character level
- perhaps sqrt(CLvl). i.e. a character must advance to Clvl 9 before
an armor with a +3 enchantment can be randomly generated.


Frankly, I think all of the above ideas are terrible, and you have
embarked on a fools errand. However, the topic does raise some
interesting points for discussion.

In particular, I suspect that vanilla NetHack would have only a small
fraction of its current playerbase today if early game luck was
nerfed. Many newbie players would have given up the game as a bad job
if not for the RNG dangling the possibility of an early wish or a
randomly generated +5 CoMR in front of them.




